00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.730
Jack: What is meant by
an Agile development?

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.469
We have already mentioned ‘Agile’ in
previous units but now let’s look at it

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.850
in more detail and see how this process
is used for software development.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.499
Leah: ‘Agile Development’ which is
also more commonly known as Agile

00:00:17.649 --> 00:00:21.800
Software Development, is an umbrella
term for a range of frameworks,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.330
such as ‘Scrum’, and practices such
as the ‘daily stand-up meeting’.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.982
It adopts the principles set
out in the ‘Agile Manifesto’

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:32.136
and the ‘Agile 12 Principles’.

00:00:32.136 --> 00:00:36.680
Agile came about as a process to
address issues that software developers

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.100
faced, focussing on a project being
flexible, so it could respond to any

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.550
changes and deal with any uncertainty.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.940
Jack: Agile is people-oriented and
result-focused in respect to software

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.609
delivery and instead of delivering all
the product at the end of the project,

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:56.160
the software is built incrementally
from the start in time-boxed 'sprints'.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.560
It is an iterative approach used in
software development projects with a

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.200
focus on continuous releases that takes
customer feedback into consideration.

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:07.190
So, how does this work?

00:01:08.469 --> 00:01:12.709
Development tasks are broken down into
bite size chunks, which can be achieved

00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:14.770
in a certain period known as a ‘sprint’.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.550
The agile team itself would normally
consist of different skillsets, working

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.990
independently and collaboratively, to
achieve the tasks within that set time.

00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:29.690
Leah: Let’s now look at the ‘Agile
Manifesto’ and how it originated.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:34.230
The Agile Manifesto is a short document,
which was created in 2001 from a group of

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.180
leading professionals who met to discuss
the future of software development.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.789
Its focus is on value to individuals and
interactions, working software, customer

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:45.940
collaboration and responding to change.

00:01:46.449 --> 00:01:50.860
The Manifesto itself is short and
made up of 68 words and has since

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.610
been embraced by many different teams
and companies, and it is agreed by

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.690
many that it is still relevant today.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.680
Jack: What are the 12 Principles?

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:04.230
The 12 Principles of Agile Software,
expand on the values within the Manifesto.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.310
The focus is on the customer,
continuous delivery of product,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:14.110
change, frequency, team collaboration,
motivation, support, communication,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.359
measurement, excellence, simplicity,
self-organisation, and reflection.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.200
To read more about this, see
the full Manifesto and the 12

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.720
principles in the document and
weblinks provided below this video.

